## Proof

Let $f(z) = \sum_{n=0}^{\infty} a_n z^n$ be the Taylor expansion of $f$ about $0$, valid on $D$.

For any $r \in (0,1)$, the Cauchy estimate on the circle $|z| = r$ gives
$$
|a_n| \le \frac{M(r)}{r^n}, \qquad M(r) := \max_{|z|=r} |f(z)|.
$$
By hypothesis $|f(z)| \le 1 - |z|$, so on $|z| = r$ we have $|f(z)| \le 1 - r$, hence $M(r) \le 1 - r$. Therefore, for every $r \in (0,1)$ and every $n \ge 0$,
$$
|a_n| \le \frac{1 - r}{r^n}.
$$

Now fix $n \ge 0$ and let $r \to 1^{-}$.

- For $n = 0$: $\dfrac{1-r}{r^0} = 1 - r \to 0$, so $|a_0| \le 0$, giving $a_0 = 0$.
- For $n \ge 1$: $r^n \to 1$ as $r \to 1^{-}$, so $\dfrac{1-r}{r^n} \to 0$, giving $|a_n| \le 0$, hence $a_n = 0$.

Thus $a_n = 0$ for every $n \ge 0$. Since $f$ is analytic on the disk, its Taylor series converges to $f$ everywhere in $D$, so
$$
f(z) = \sum_{n=0}^{\infty} a_n z^n = 0 \quad \text{for all } z \in D.
$$

Therefore $f \equiv 0$ on $D$. $\blacksquare$

